    {
      "question": "How does it differ from the HAZ?",
      "status": "complete",
      "browser_total_ms": 1145.0,
      "browser_first_visible_ms": 1139.6,
      "backend_total_ms": 625,
      "timing_caption": "Search 9 ms \u00b7 Rerank 141 ms \u00b7 Generate 474 ms \u00b7 First text 625 ms \u00b7 Total 625 ms",
      "answer_words": 106,
      "answer": "TMAZ (Thermo\u2011Mechanically Affected Zone)\nThe TMAZ is the region adjacent to the Stir Zone (SZ) that experiences plastic deformation and temperature rise during friction stir welding. It is plastically deformed but does not recrystallize, resulting in an inhomogeneous microstructure and a hardness minimum relative to the SZ. [1][2]\n\nDifference from the HAZ (Heat\u2011Affected Zone)\nThe HAZ lies further from the SZ and is affected only by the heat generated during welding, without significant plastic deformation. Consequently, the HAZ typically shows a different microstructural evolution (e.g., grain growth or coarsening) and a distinct hardness profile compared to the TMAZ, which is dominated by mechanical stirring effects. [2]",
